# OpenAPI Endpoint Descriptions

Descriptions are read by **client developers and AI agents building integrations**. Write for them — not for someone reading the backend source code.

## Principles

- **What, not how**: Describe what the endpoint does from the caller's perspective. Never mention implementation details (database tables, internal service names, queue workers).
- **Input/output clarity**: State what the caller must send and what they get back. If a field is nullable, say when it is null.
- **Behaviour callers care about**: idempotency, error codes, ordering, rate limits, async patterns.
- **Cross-references**: Link related endpoints by path (e.g. `GET /recognitions/{id}`).
- **No internal storage concepts**: Do not expose internal data model distinctions (e.g. "global place", "user-specific place", "saved collection"). The client only knows about IDs returned by API responses — use plain language like "your saved places" or "the place" instead.

## Format

- Use plain prose for simple endpoints (1–2 sentences).
- Use `### Heading` + bullet lists only when there are genuinely distinct modes or multiple steps a caller must understand.
- Keep descriptions under ~20 lines unless the endpoint has complex branching behaviour.

## ❌ Bad (backend internals leaking)

```ts
description: "Calls Google Places Details API if the place row is missing; upserts into places table."
description: "Removes from saved_places. Global place data shared across users is not affected."
description: "Returns the ID of your personal save, not the canonical place identifier."
```

## ✅ Good (client-facing)

```ts
description: "Saves a place. Full details are fetched automatically — no extra call needed from the client."
description: "Removes this place from your saved places and any lists it was added to."
description: "Returns a single saved place. Use the `place_id` returned by `POST /places` or `GET /places`."
```

## Checklist before committing an openapi.ts change

- [ ] No mention of table names, column names, or internal service names
- [ ] Async flows explain how to poll or subscribe for results
- [ ] Discriminated-union request bodies explain each variant
- [ ] Nullable fields say when they are null
- [ ] Error codes (409, 422…) beyond the common set are documented
